+# Extreme Heat Decision Memo
+## Decision
+
+Metroville should make a voluntary cool-roof rebate for Ward 2 multifamily buildings its lead extreme-heat policy for renter households during the next 18 months. Voluntary right-of-way tree planting should be the complementary policy, concentrated along renter-used sidewalks and near renter-majority buildings. Roof work can reach occupied buildings within the stated period, while trees begin a longer-lived layer of outdoor shade.
+
+## Evidence
+
+Ward 2 has a clear exposure and equity case for focused action. Renters make up 68% of its households, versus 44% citywide, while tree canopy covers only 11% of the ward, compared with 24% citywide. The ward experienced 42 days at or above 90 degrees Fahrenheit in 2025 and logged 31 heat-related service requests per 10,000 residents, nearly twice the citywide rate of 17 per 10,000 [S1]. Service requests indicate reported need rather than a complete count of heat illness.
+
+The building stock makes a roof strategy feasible. Seventy-one percent of Ward 2 renter households live in buildings with at least five units, and 63% of multifamily buildings have flat or low-slope roofs potentially suitable for reflective materials [S3]. A local demonstration measured an average 7-degree-Fahrenheit reduction in sampled summer roof-surface temperature after a reflective roof was installed [S1]. That single project supports the physical mechanism, not a ward-wide air-temperature promise. Federal guidance likewise says cool roofs reduce absorbed solar heat, with results varying by roof, building, weather, and installation [S2].
+
+Tree evidence supports a parallel but longer-term role. The ward has 1,120 plantable right-of-way sites, but only 26% are within 50 feet of renter-majority multifamily property [S4]. Young trees usually need 3 to 5 growing seasons to provide substantial shade [S4]. Trees therefore address public routes and future canopy gaps more readily than immediate indoor exposure.
+
+## Options
+
+The cool-roof option offers a near-term benefit at the building scale: less roof heat can reduce heat transfer into qualifying properties. Its equity advantage is that one participating owner can reach many renters without requiring each tenant to arrange an upgrade. Feasibility is supported by capacity for as many as 140 qualifying projects in 18 months and prior assessment participation by 54% of contacted owners when technical help and tenant notices were supplied [S3]. Its limitations matter. Benefits are most direct for upper-floor units and roof-driven heat, technical assessment is still necessary, and owner participation cannot be assumed [S5]. A voluntary program must therefore measure actual tenant reach rather than equating owner interest with reduced indoor exposure.
+
+Right-of-way planting offers a different benefit: shade on sidewalks and shared routes is available regardless of whether someone rents or owns. It also builds canopy where the ward is far below the city average. The parks department can plant and place 420 trees into establishment care within 18 months [S4], making initial delivery feasible. Yet meaningful shade arrives beyond the decision window, and site distribution limits direct reach to renter-majority buildings. Survival is also an equity and performance concern: the three-year rate was 78% with scheduled establishment watering but 52% without it [S4]. Planting counts alone would overstate success.
+
+## Recommendation
+
+Lead with cool roofs because the renter-heavy multifamily stock and available delivery network align with an 18-month response. Enrollment should remain voluntary for property owners, but outreach should begin with suitable buildings on the hottest blocks and explain that roof work follows an on-site assessment. The city should provide the technical assistance and plain-language tenant notice used in earlier outreach. Tenant communication should state expected disruption and avoid guaranteeing identical indoor results, reflecting residents' expressed preferences and the variation among buildings [S5].
+
+Use tree planting as the complement, prioritizing plantable sites near renter-majority properties, transit walks, and other heavily used routes. Scheduled establishment care should accompany every planted tree because the survival evidence makes care part of the intervention, not an optional follow-on. This combination keeps both policies voluntary while matching each to what it can credibly deliver: earlier building-level heat reduction from roofs and expanding outdoor shade from trees.
+
+## Implementation
+
+During months 1 through 3, map technically promising multifamily roofs against Ward 2 heat indicators, open voluntary owner outreach, and establish a tenant-facing survey baseline. At the same time, rank right-of-way sites by proximity to renter-majority buildings and common walking routes. Report the number of buildings assessed, their unit counts, and their block distribution rather than treating inquiries as completed reach.
+
+During months 4 through 12, complete qualified roof projects and begin planting in the ranked locations. Track completed roofs, renter households in participating buildings, tenant notice delivery, and post-work tenant reports of top-floor comfort. Publish results with the warning that self-reported comfort and roof-surface change do not establish neighborhood air-temperature change [S1][S5].
+
+By month 18, report progress against two delivery outcomes: up to 140 completed qualifying roof projects and 420 trees placed into scheduled establishment care [S3][S4]. Also publish the share of tree sites near renter-majority buildings, establishment-care completion, early survival observations, and survey response coverage. Council can then judge reach, implementation quality, and distribution while preserving longer-term tracking of tree survival and tenant experience.
